A||||11||mgbringo||86 GT won't start||||1||
Z||000000||mgbringo||12-30-2001||06:08 PM||||What should I check?<P>It is a 76500 mile, very nice car.  Has been very reliable until yesterday.  I have also tuned it up over this past summer with new plugs, cap, rotor, wires.  Has been running great.<P>I started it to back it out of the garage and it took about 10 seconds to start.  It has always started very quickly in the past.  It ran well though so I just backed it out and shut it off.<P>Came to put it back in the garage and it will not start.  Turns over very well but no sign of starting.<P>What do I check?  I will go out now to see if it has spark.  What are the most likely causes?<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||mgbringo||12-30-2001||06:21 PM||||An update.  I just tried it again and it started but ran real rough.  <P>Then quit and wouldn't start again.  Then a few minutes later, same thing.<P>I hooked up a remote spark tester into one wire and the spark seems to come and go.  Has spark for a while and then no spark.<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||88CoupeV6||12-30-2001||06:27 PM||||Can you hear the fuel pump humming when you turn the key on?  If it has no spark could be the module in the Dist.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88coupeV6.jpg">       <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>    <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/nefa.htm" TARGET=_blank>Join New England Fiero Association</A>||||reg||3
Z||000003||mgbringo||12-30-2001||07:09 PM||||Yes, I can hear the fuel pump running.<P>Guess I will buy a new module tomorrow.  Recommend going to GM dealer for that?  Could it be the coil?  I can get that tested, right?<P>Thanks for your quick response.<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||PBJ||12-30-2001||07:21 PM||||Ignition modules are test-able. If you remove it, I know our shop has the ability to test it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hairy_Fiero||12-30-2001||07:52 PM||||Sounds like it could be one of three things since you said the spark comes and goes. This means it's most like an electrical problem and not fuel or air. Ignition module, ignition coil or the ignition pickup coil. In most cases it's either the module or ignition coil, however the pickup coil can also cause this problem. <P>You mentioned the car ran rough when you got it started, this really makes me lean towards your ignition coil being the problem. I'd buy a new ignition coil and replace your old one. When the coil won't hold a steady voltage it will cause the engine to stumble, and is most likely the cause of your problems. Good luck let us know what you do/find out!<p>[This message has been edited by Hairy_Fiero (edited 12-30-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000006||mgbringo||12-30-2001||09:20 PM||||Thanks for the info.  You guys are great.  <P>I am going to buy a module and a coil tomorrow.  I will let you know how it goes.  <P>Thanks again and Happy New Year.<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||mgbringo||12-31-2001||12:35 PM||||Where is the pickup coil?   I replaced the ignition coil and the module inside the distributor and it still won't start.<P>Is the pickup coil the part inside the distributor around the center shaft?  Mine looks corroded.<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||Hairy_Fiero||12-31-2001||01:18 PM||||Yes, that's your pickup coil. Should be 7-15 dollars from your local parts place. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||RossT||12-31-2001||05:00 PM||||Here is a link on how to replace pickup coil.<BR>click on articles then scroll down.<BR> <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/</A> <P><BR>Did you check your fuel filter?  It still could be your fuel pump.  Make sure you are getting good fuel presure.  May be a good time to replace the pump anyway at 77,000 miles.  If you do, make sure you use only AC delco pump!! Ive done it twice if you need help,,<P>Good luck.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||SKIDMARK||12-31-2001||05:44 PM||||Are you %100 sure the spark is intermittant? Check again to make sure you have a spark problem. The pick up coil is just that, a coil. The only 2 things that go wrong with it are the wire breaks or gets shorted out. In either case the car won't run or fire at all. The other components you have already changed so chances are, they are good.<BR>Another thing to check would be the temperature sensor. It can corode and fool the computer into thinking the engine is warm causing no enrichment and no start cold.<BR>(I assume it's cold where you live)<BR>Good luck with it and let us know what it turns out to be.||||reg||1
Z||000011||mgbringo||01-01-2002||12:24 AM||||Update....<P>I picked up a new pickup coil from local Chevy dealer for $18.   Marked the distributor and removed it.  Knocked out the pin and removed the gear.<P>Couldn't get the shaft out.  Tried everything.  Finally got it out by pounding it.  Big mistake, the bushing came with it.<P>How does that bushing stay in the housing?  It is the bushing that the clip for the pickup coil fits into.  I then noticed that the ceramic, or whatever it is, on the top of the shaft was broken and falling out.<P>Looks like I will be buying a rebuilt distributor?  Is the shaft available seperately?  <P>Too bad because the distributor seemed really tight with no play in shaft.  I am pretty sure the problem is the pickup coil.  I can see the wire insulation is craked in a few places and the one wire was broken.<P>I have replaced the fuel filter but not the pump.  I am pretty sure it is a spark problem though.  Got spark a couple of times only for a couple of sparks and then it would stop again.  Most of the time I was trouble shooting it had no spark.<P>Any other ideas for me?  <P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero GT<p>[This message has been edited by mgbringo (edited 01-01-2002).]||||reg||1
